#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483509} :activity/id #uuid "0521a0d5-f5e3-5c81-bc28-29faeb852deb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483432 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bebf495e-8b3b-5a9d-a588-759127f24c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435704}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435704} :answer-field/id #uuid "dff647f1-983f-5ba6-9928-87a2fdcf179e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e7130a2f-a2af-518c-8ac7-89bf1a4dc82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-343673" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-4sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky-4sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=-8sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-8sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{3}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "03961e7e-a825-52c6-937e-d6714eaba4ff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483466}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "498bed50-28c7-50fa-a5df-0080495261c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441791} :answer-field/id #uuid "5abd7c36-14a4-56ef-ada9-6c206b80a9d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "968f331f-040e-5a16-a459-23a91c08dfd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343473" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5ba3c2fb-de20-5932-96c6-e28e4bb83716" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483432}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483466 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08119a57-31c4-59b9-8f0b-94a273b4bb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467256} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d69105-ab2d-55c3-9612-ee987e634694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467256} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d8b9ffd-965a-5c5b-8676-57da40fbb9a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468349} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31720ab0-69d4-52f5-b3cb-fcd1e2ec0d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467258} {:answer/id #uuid "1532792a-3167-5d17-8ad8-5abe35456b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467259} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b86baf-0e5a-524c-b103-8dac5824fae3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468350} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0585d6a-1830-5479-8da2-a0cc0d62a4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467260} {:answer/id #uuid "6239f5bd-e810-5e80-93e2-7e27a749a934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467260} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad5a3574-1760-5b93-87fd-b5f37a0870f3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9cf1f6c6-b17b-5c90-a172-f49c17abaa8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343746" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[16(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 16D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{8}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nOnly solution II matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "65d89567-4925-56ab-9bcd-c0152afe9d34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8588f05c-f259-5f6f-95a4-ba743efb8633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230323} {:answer/id #uuid "87e2a5ac-5e6b-5176-ada3-76165f3827b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230324} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0f684b-31be-53dd-959a-4c693200fbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230325} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbe1595-b0a0-5005-89d3-b1a66671386d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230326} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad71e63-ab40-5319-b40d-20fec2c9d4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230327}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230326} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6c1fc5b-ecb6-509b-ab8e-ba64d464ed59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2dc0571-7d63-58c4-a7e9-b264e1dac3a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343760" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $D=1.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} :step/id #uuid "aa755d32-f5f0-5147-8e10-608442403c58"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105f4c4e-d4f2-5528-bfdd-59865716d537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315218} :answer-field/id #uuid "5551b954-fda2-5f2d-abc7-527dd2646792" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f6ac78e-cb74-5768-92ef-290d5885de7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343459" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),$ which is $5,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fe5e8487-7f26-563a-8df2-2908b3b409a8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483460}}] :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482797}]}
